Approach: Use Floyd’s Cycle Detection to find the meeting point of slow and fast pointers. Then move one pointer from head and the other from the meeting point; the node where they meet again is the starting node of the cycle.

Time Complexity: O(n) — cycle detection and finding the cycle's starting node together take linear time.

Space Complexity: O(1) — only pointer variables are used, no extra data structures.

public class Solution {
    public ListNode detectCycle(ListNode head) {
         ListNode slow = head, fast = head;
        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;{
            if (slow == fast) 
            break;
            }
        }
        if (fast == null || fast.next == null) {
            return null;
        }
        while (head != slow) {
            head = head.next;
            slow = slow.next;
        }
        return head;
    }
}
